# The minimap can be paged to a level the player is not standing on (#559).
# Page Up / Page Down / Home are unreachable from a terminal, so the ASCII
# alt bindings , . and / are what a --text player uses — this script drives
# exactly those (see alt_minimap_level_up / _down / _reset in
# src/config/keyBindings.py).
#
# The tracked config.yml ships `showMiniMap: true`, so the minimap is up from
# the moment the world is entered and the paging keys are live. Assertions
# stay ASCII-only: the status line and the minimap caption both contain an
# em dash, which is not worth matching through a pty.
expect Roam
send enter
expect Select Save
send c
expect Enter save name
send {saveName} enter
expect Tick timeout=15
# A fresh save starts on the surface, so paging down shows the cave below
# without the player descending; the depth indicator stays absent because the
# player themselves is still at z = 0.
send .
assertContains "Map: cave level 1"
assertContains "Viewing cave level 1"
# The level below is unvisited, so the room grid shows the player's column as
# '+' rather than their facing arrow.
send ,
assertContains "Map: surface (your level)"
# Paging stops at the deepest level the world generates rather than running on.
send . . . .
assertContains "Map already at cave level 3"
# One press of the reset key comes back to the level being stood on. The
# buffer is reset first so the match is new output rather than the earlier
# surface message still sitting in the transcript.
resetBuffer
send /
assertContains "Map: surface (your level)"
